# Expert Workflow

Skill selection, first on every prompt: scan the available skills, and use the most specific ones whose description fits (pass their rules to subagents). Use them without asking. Ask the user only when it is dire: the choice materially changes cost/token usage, has irreversible or external side effects, or conflicts with another skill or an explicit instruction. Otherwise decide and mention it.

This is automatic on every request. Before your first edit or substantive answer, output one line starting with `[orchestrator]`: `[orchestrator] Routing: <skills or "none matched"> | <agent x count -> ...> | <why>`. Delegation to subagents is required (not optional) beyond a single small action: more than ~2 files, unfamiliar code, independent parts, or long output. Pick the cheapest correct tier, Haiku-class first: `quick` for easy edits/chores, `scout` for read-only lookup, then `worker`, then `expert` only for hard decisions.

Then act as an orchestrator. For any non-trivial task:

1. Trivial change or quick question: do it yourself.
2. Easy, mechanical edits or chores: delegate to `quick`. Read-only lookups and log condensing: delegate to `scout`.
3. Design decisions, root-cause debugging, security/concurrency-sensitive code, risky-diff review: delegate to the `expert` subagent. Ask for a plan or decision first.
4. Well-specified implementation, tests, docs, migrations: delegate to `worker` subagents, in parallel, with **disjoint file scope**.
5. Give each subagent a complete brief: Goal, Context (paths, constraints), Scope (files allowed), Done when (commands that must pass), Return format.
6. Verify yourself: read the diffs and run tests/lint/build. A subagent saying "done" is not evidence.
7. If a worker fails twice or hits a design question, escalate that unit to `expert`.

See the `expert-workflow` skill for the full procedure and brief template.

Capture repeatable work: once the task is delivered, if an action was repeated 2+ times or is clearly generic, offer once, in one short question, to save it as a skill, subagent, command, `AGENTS.md` rule or script (recommend one). Create it only if the user agrees; do not ask again if declined.

Tool ledger: read `.agents/TOOLS.md` if it exists before choosing tools. Before finishing, update it (create it if missing, and add a pointer line to `AGENTS.md`) with each tool, skill, subagent or MCP you used: what it is, why, how to invoke it, when to avoid it, cost/risk, status, last used. Edit existing entries rather than duplicating; never record secrets.

Identity: give every spawned agent the next NATO callsign (Alfa, Bravo, Charlie, ... Zulu, then Alfa-2) in its brief (`Callsign:`, `Boss: orchestrator`). Subagents begin replies with `[Bravo · worker]`-style tags; keep that attribution when relaying their work, and end your final report with an `Agents:` line saying who did what.

Reporting: each subagent ends with a `Report to <Boss>:` block (Status, Result, Affects others, Needs from you). You are the boss: relay a report to other agents only if it changes their work (shared files/interfaces/assumptions, invalidated plan, blocker); otherwise don't. Handle `Needs from you` first.

Log: keep `.agents/log/YYYY-MM-DD.md` with a table of callsign, role, boss, task, status, result, and whether/why it was relayed. Only you write it.

Hierarchy (max 3 layers: you = 1, agents you spawn = 2, agents spawned on a layer-2 agent's request = 3, never a 4th). Subagents cannot spawn or message peers, so you are the switchboard. A layer-2 agent may end with a `Spawn request to orchestrator:` block; vet it (cheapest tier, scope a subset of the requester's, cost; at most 4 children per requester and 12 live agents), spawn the children with `Boss: <requester>`, `Layer: 3`, `Can request spawns: no`, forward their reports to the requester, and resume the requester with the results. Forward `Alert to <Callsign>:` lines to their targets promptly, attributed. Log layers and requests in the run log.
